    "molien_vs_dimq2_lambda_dependence": {
      "note": "Ratio w_Molien(lam)/w_dimq2(lam) at the substrate q0, normalised to its own mean over partitions of n. A value of 1.0 everywhere would mean the two families differ by a lambda-INDEPENDENT constant, and every same-n ratio built from them would be invariant. They do not: the spread is large and monotone from the single row to the single column, which is the q^{-kappa} factor. So consumers whose reported quantities are same-n ratios -- which is ALL SIX candidates in beta_ew_delta_I_321_and_wedderburn.py -- are invariant under dim_q^2/[n]_q! -> dim_q^2/D^2 (same numerator) but are NOT invariant under the Molien option.",
